  "id": "DrugBank_DB00413 - MONDO_10482",
  "verbalization": "Pramipexole is implicated in the pathogenesis of X-linked Parkinsonism-spasticity Syndrome through a chain of indications that link it to hereditary late-onset Parkinson disease. This syndrome is characterized by a progressive loss of dopaminergic function, and Pramipexole, a dopamine agonist, is known to be used in the treatment of Parkinson disease. The evidence suggests that Pramipexole may exert a therapeutic effect on X-linked Parkinsonism-spasticity Syndrome through a mechanism involving the modulation of dopaminergic pathways, potentially by enhancing dopaminergic neurotransmission or by influencing downstream signaling pathways. The presence of multiple pathways—such as the use of Pramipexole in conjunction with Istradefylline, Tolcapone, and Pimavanserin—indicates a broader role in the management of dopaminergic disorders, which may contribute to the observed relationship. While the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated, the integrated evidence suggests a possible connection between Pramipexole and X-linked Parkinsonism-spasticity Syndrome through its role in dopaminergic modulation.",
